To trigger an event handler manually for your buttons in jQuery, you can use the `.trigger()` method [CHNK_PRIMARY]. 

First, bind an event handler to your element using the `.on()` method, and then call `.trigger()` with the name of the event (such as `"click"`) [CHNK_PRIMARY]:

```javascript
// Bind a click event handler to the button
$( "#foo" ).on( "click" , function ( ) { 
    alert( $( this ).text() ); 
}); 

// Manually trigger the click event
$( "#foo" ).trigger( "click" );
```

### Key Details:
* **Execution Order:** Calling `.trigger()` executes the attached handlers in the same order they would run if the event were triggered naturally by the user [CHNK_PRIMARY].
* **Custom Events:** You can also define and trigger custom events, passing extra parameters to the handler if needed [CHNK_PRIMARY]:
  ```javascript
  $( "#foo" ).on( "custom" , function ( event, param1, param2 ) { 
      alert( param1 + " " + param2 ); 
  }); 
  $( "#foo" ).trigger( "custom" , [ "Custom" , "Event" ] );
  ```
* **Avoiding Native Events:** To trigger the jQuery-bound handlers without triggering the browser's native event behavior, use `.triggerHandler()` instead [CHNK_PRIMARY].
